 Overview
 ========
 
-`alevin-fry`` is a suite of tools for the rapid, accurate and memory-frugal processing single-cell and single-nucleus sequencing data. It consumes RAD files generated by `salmon alevin`, and performs common operations like generating permit lists, and estimating the number of distinct molecules from each gene within each cell. The focus in `alevin-fry`` is on safety, accuracy and efficiency (in terms of both time and memory usage).
+`alevin-fry`` is a suite of tools for the rapid, accurate and memory-frugal processing single-cell and single-nucleus sequencing data. It consumes RAD files generated by `piscem` or `salmon alevin`, and performs common operations like generating permit lists, and estimating the number of distinct molecules from each gene within each cell. The focus in `alevin-fry`` is on safety, accuracy and efficiency (in terms of both time and memory usage).
 
 You can read the paper describing alevin fry, "Alevin-fry unlocks rapid, accurate, and memory-frugal quantification of single-cell RNA-seq data" `in Nature Methods <https://www.nature.com/articles/s41592-022-01408-3>`_, and the pre-print `on bioRxiv <https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2021.06.29.450377v1>`_.
 
+Compatibility
+-------------
+
+Since piscem 0.24, the RAD files written by `piscem` use version 2 of the RAD format (self-describing: a ``RAD_FILE`` magic, a format version and tag roles). `salmon alevin` and piscem 0.23 or earlier write RAD v1.
+
+* `alevin-fry` releases after 0.18.3 read both RAD v1 and RAD v2.
+* `alevin-fry` 0.18.3 and earlier read only RAD v1, so they cannot process the output of piscem 0.24 or later. They fail while parsing the RAD header, and the message does not mention versions: ``generate-permit-list`` panics with e.g. ``invalid utf-8 sequence of 1 bytes from index ...``, and ``atac generate-permit-list`` with ``could not create ParallelRadReader: could not parse the RAD prelude``. If you see these errors, check the `alevin-fry` version first; ``head -c 8 map.rad`` prints ``RAD_FILE`` for a RAD v2 file. (The same errors can also mean a truncated or corrupt RAD file.)
+
